```console
$ useful workflow run resume --help
Continue a run from where it stands, rerunning nothing

Usage: useful workflow run resume [OPTIONS] <RUN>

Arguments:
  <RUN>  Run directory, run ID or subject

Options:
      --workflow <FILE>        Replacement workflow file within the run's original source root, for a run under the useful home
      --execution-root <PATH>  Existing execution root, for a run under the useful home; defaults to the previous attempt's root
      --org <ORG>              Organization ID or exact slug to act in (default: USEFUL_ORG, then the project of this checkout, then your only organization)
      --plain                  Force plain human presentation
      --json                   Print the workflow result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>           Select renderer color behavior [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --api-key-file <PATH|->  Authenticate with a service API key from a private file, or - for standard input
      --allow-insecure-http    Allow this command's Useful Machinery requests over insecure HTTP connections
  -h, --help                   Print help

RUN is a run directory, a run ID or a subject. An ID or a subject is looked up under the useful home and, when the home holds no such run, in Cloud in the organization in effect.

A run under the home continues once a control resolves its wait, and otherwise the command reports the wait unchanged; a run whose process died continues from the step it was running, and one whose latest attempt chained continues from the edge it took. An ended run is not resumed: the command refuses it, naming the commands that apply, useful workflow run retry among them unless the run succeeded.

For a Cloud run the command asks Cloud to continue the run under its own definition from where its record stands, and reports the request. It takes no --workflow or --execution-root.

```
